sched/fair: defer the avg_vruntime overflow WARN to avoid rq->lock self-deadlock

A hard LOCKUP was observed on ~130 hosts (6.13 fbk16, AMD Turin, 252 CPUs).
The NMI watchdog fired on a CPU spinning in queued_spin_lock_slowpath ->
raw_spin_rq_lock -> sched_balance_rq -> _nohz_idle_balance, but that CPU is
only a victim: 52 CPUs were queued on one runqueue lock (CPU 38's rq).

The real holder, CPU 38, self-deadlocked. It was in __schedule() holding its
own rq->__lock and, deep inside pick_next_task_fair() ->
put_prev_entity() -> __enqueue_entity() -> __sum_w_vruntime_add(), the
avg_vruntime overflow WARN_ON_ONCE() at fair.c:691 fired (key*weight had bit
62 set: w_vruntime = -6865589408623720101, weight = 0x1ca3fd). Because this
WARN executes with rq->__lock held, its printk was emitted *synchronously*
through the wake-capable netconsole path:

  WARN -> printk -> console_unlock -> console_emit_next_record ->
  netpoll_send_udp -> netpoll_poll_dev -> bnxt_poll_p5 -> __bnxt_tx_int ->
  dev_kfree_skb_irq_reason -> raise_softirq_irqoff -> wakeup_softirqd ->
  wake_up_process(ksoftirqd/38) -> try_to_wake_up -> ttwu_queue ->
  rq_lock(CPU38 rq) -> queued_spin_lock_slowpath (spins forever)

try_to_wake_up() tried to re-acquire the very rq->__lock CPU 38 already held,
so the lock was never released. Every other CPU that picked CPU 38's rq as the
"busiest" in nohz idle balance then piled up behind it with IRQs disabled,
which in turn stalled TLB-shootdown IPIs to those CPUs (csd_lock_wait_toolong
in dmesg), until the NMI watchdog declared a hard lockup.

The scheduler already knows printk under rq->__lock is unsafe and uses
printk_deferred*() for its own messages (see kernel/sched/psi.c,
kernel/sched/core.c, kernel/sched/fair.c:5870). This experimental
avg_vruntime instrumentation was the one rq-lock-held diagnostic that did not.
Wrap the WARN in printk_deferred_enter()/exit() so console output is deferred
(defer_console_output() / irq_work) instead of emitted synchronously; IRQs are
already disabled here, satisfying the printk_deferred requirement. The WARN is
still logged - only the dangerous synchronous console re-entry is removed.

Note: the underlying avg_vruntime key*weight overflow (the condition the WARN
detects) is a separate, still-open issue in the experimental EEVDF avg_vruntime
rework and is being addressed upstream (see c06e05dae2c6 "sched/fair: Avoid
overflow in enqueue_entity()", 3f374515315d "sched/fair: Increase weight bits
for avg_vruntime", both already present). This patch fixes the fatal mechanism
(the fleet-wide lockup); it does not by itself stop the overflow from
recurring.

diff --git a/kernel/sched/fair.c b/kernel/sched/fair.c
index 5dc612effbec..14a5f8196b11 100644
--- a/kernel/sched/fair.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/fair.c
@@ -688,7 +688,17 @@ __sum_w_vruntime_add(struct cfs_rq *cfs_rq, struct sched_entity *se)
 	s64 w_vruntime, key = entity_key(cfs_rq, se);

 	w_vruntime = key * weight;
+	/*
+	 * This runs under rq->__lock with IRQs disabled (pick/enqueue paths).
+	 * A plain WARN here emits printk synchronously; with a wake-capable
+	 * console (netconsole -> netpoll TX cleanup -> wake_up_process()) that
+	 * re-enters try_to_wake_up() and self-deadlocks on the rq->__lock we
+	 * already hold. Defer console output like the other scheduler
+	 * diagnostics (see printk_deferred* users in kernel/sched/).
+	 */
+	printk_deferred_enter();
 	WARN_ON_ONCE((w_vruntime >> 63) != (w_vruntime >> 62));
+	printk_deferred_exit();

 	cfs_rq->sum_w_vruntime += w_vruntime;
 	cfs_rq->sum_weight += weight;
